// DocsDrivers: Pluggable execution driver architecture guides
---

<h2 id="dual-engine">Dual-Engine Fleet Architecture</h2>
<p>
  Unlike traditional runner solutions that force you into a single execution
  model, RunZero offers a pluggable driver architecture:
</p>

<h3 id="orbstack-driver">
  🪐 OrbStack Linux VM Driver (<code>RUNNER_BACKEND=orbstack-vm</code>)
</h3>
<p>
  Spawns dedicated lightweight Linux machines via <a
    href="https://orbstack.dev"
    target="_blank"
    rel="noopener">OrbStack</a
  > and Apple Virtualization.framework. Provides <strong
    >full systemd support</strong
  >, unconfined Google Chrome sandboxing, background daemons, and internal
  Docker-in-VM capabilities. Automatically connects to host Verdaccio, Athens,
  and apt-cacher proxies via <code>host.orb.internal</code>.
</p>

<h3 id="golden-vm">
  🏗️ Golden VM Base Image (<code>make build-vm-base</code>)
</h3>
<p>
  To make VM boot times near-instant, <code>make build-vm-base</code> pre-bakes the
  shared toolchain into a golden base image (<code
    >runzero-vm-base-&lt;arch&gt;</code
  >). Subsequent VM jobs spin up in seconds by cloning the base image!
</p>

<h3 id="docker-driver">
  🐳 Docker Container Driver (<code>RUNNER_BACKEND=docker</code>)
</h3>
<p>
  Launches ultra-fast ephemeral containers powered by <a
    href="https://docs.docker.com/engine/"
    target="_blank"
    rel="noopener">Docker Engine</a
  >. Starts in <strong>~0.3 seconds</strong> and consumes zero RAM when idle. Ideal
  for unit tests, linting, build pipelines, and JavaScript/Python workflows.
</p>

<h3 id="wsl-driver">
  🪟 Windows WSL2 Driver (<code>RUNNER_BACKEND=wsl2</code>)
</h3>
<p>
  Runs native Linux runners directly inside <a
    href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/wsl/"
    target="_blank"
    rel="noopener">Windows Subsystem for Linux 2</a
  > (Windows 10/11 and Windows Server) without requiring Docker Desktop.
</p>

<h3 id="multipass-driver">
  🚀 Canonical Multipass Driver (<code>RUNNER_BACKEND=multipass</code>)
</h3>
<p>
  Universal cross-platform virtual machine backend using <a
    href="https://multipass.run/"
    target="_blank"
    rel="noopener">Canonical Multipass</a
  > via QEMU, Hyper-V, or VirtualBox.
</p>
